sis of bandlimited signals often faces a trade-off between the computational efficiency of the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT) and the precision of Discrete Prolate Spheroidal Sequences (DPSS), or Slepian vectors. While DPSS provides a highly compact representation that minimizes frequency leakage, its $O(N^2)$ computational complexity is prohibitive for large-scale applications. This paper introduces the Rapid Orthogonal Approximate Slepian Transform (ROAST), a fast, FFT-like algorithm that provides an orthonormal basis approximating the DPSS subspace. ROAST achieves the accuracy of Slepian-based methods with $O(N \\log N)$ complexity, offering a well-conditioned alternative to previous fast Slepian transforms.\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem provides the foundation for representing bandlimited signals through discrete samples. \n## The ROAST Framework\n\nROAST is designed to bridge the gap between the speed of the FFT and the concentration properties of DPSS, while strictly maintaining an orthonormal basis. The core insight of the ROAST framework is that the difference between a standard partial DFT matrix and the ideal DPSS basis is mathematically \"low-rank.\"\n\n### Methodology\n\nThe ROAST basis is constructed by concatenating two distinct components:\n\n  1. Partial DFT Matrix: This component captures the majority of the low-frequency energy. Because it is a subset of the DFT, it can be applied rapidly using standard FFT algorithms.\n  2. Low-Rank Correction Matrix ($V$): To account for the frequency leakage and the \"missing\" information not captured by the partial DFT, the authors append a small number of carefully selected vectors. These vectors are derived from the dominant singular vectors of the residual matrix (the difference between the DPSS projection and the partial DFT projection).\n\nTo further enhance efficiency, the authors introduce ROAST-R, which utilizes a randomized Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) to approximate the matrix $V$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThèse n° 7220 (https://infoscience.epfl.ch/record/279420/files/EPFL_TH7220.pdf)\nciteturn226search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; sical results of (Slepian and Pollak, 1961; Landau and Pollak, 1961, 1962) on the reconstruction ... of columns that span a near-optimal low-rank approximation to that matrix. ... the de facto standard method for solving the kernel matrix approximation problem via Fourier\nIntroduction\nSurprisingly, we show that a universal non-uniform sampling strategy can be used to solve\nthe above problem using a number of samples proportional to the statistical dimension of the\nallowed power spectrum µ. We prove that, in nearly all settings, this natural measure tightly\ncharacterizes the sample complexity of signal reconstruction. Our approach matches the clas-\nsical results of (Slepian and Pollak, 1961; Landau and Pollak, 1961, 1962) on the reconstruction\nof bandlimited signals, and extends these results to a wide class of Fourier structure types\n(with the classical setting of bandlimited signals being a special case).\nOur main technical tool is an extension of a powerful result from the randomized numerical\nlinear algebra literature to continuous Fourier operators: every matrix contains a small subset\nof columns that span a near-optimal low-rank approximation to that matrix. By extending this\nresult to continuous linear operators, we prove that the smoothness of a signal whose Fourier\ntransform has ∥x∥2\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFast approximation using Fourier Extensions (https://roelmatthysen.github.io/slides/poster_cse_roel.pdf)\nciteturn226search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Fast approximation using Fourier Extensions ... [2] D Slepian. ... well-conditioned part and low-rank ill-conditioned part.\n\n=\n\n\n\n\n,\nxi ∈